# CODEOWNERS — who must approve a change to what.
#
# Paired with the "Require review from Code Owners" branch protection rule (see
# docs/supply-chain.md §2), every pattern below becomes a mandatory reviewer on any PR
# touching a matching file. Without that rule this file only *suggests* reviewers.
#
# !! VERIFY THE HANDLE BEFORE RELYING ON THIS FILE !!
# @dhrishta2025 is taken from the repo's git config (user.name), not from GitHub. If the
# GitHub account is named differently, every rule here silently stops applying: GitHub
# flags unknown owners in the repository's CODEOWNERS view and treats those lines as
# inactive, so "Require review from Code Owners" is satisfied by nobody. Open
# Settings -> Code security, or the CODEOWNERS file view, and confirm there are no
# "Unknown owner" warnings after the first push.
#
# Order matters: the LAST matching pattern wins, so the catch-all comes first and the
# high-risk paths come after it.

# ── Default owner for everything ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*                           @dhrishta2025

# ── Authorization and tenancy: the code that decides who may see whose data ───────────────
# Every Critical in SECURITY.md §3 lived in one of these paths. Changes here need the
# matching attack suite re-run (scripts/_qa-rls-attack.mjs, scripts/_qa-tenant-integrity.mjs)
# before approval, not after merge.
/db/                        @dhrishta2025
/lib/                       @dhrishta2025
/middleware.ts              @dhrishta2025
/app/api/                   @dhrishta2025

# ── Supply chain: adding code written by strangers ───────────────────────────────────────
# A one-line package.json diff can pull in hundreds of transitive packages. Treat these as
# security review, not housekeeping — see docs/dependency-policy.md.
/package.json               @dhrishta2025
/package-lock.json          @dhrishta2025

# ── The gates themselves ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Whoever can edit CI can disable CI. These files must never be self-approvable, which is
# why "Do not allow bypassing the above settings" matters in branch protection.
/.github/                   @dhrishta2025
/.github/workflows/         @dhrishta2025
/scripts/                   @dhrishta2025

# ── Security documentation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The sign-off record. Editing it changes what the project claims about itself.
/SECURITY.md                @dhrishta2025
/THREAT-MODEL.md            @dhrishta2025
/docs/supply-chain.md       @dhrishta2025
/docs/dependency-policy.md  @dhrishta2025

# ── When a second maintainer joins ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Replace the individual handle with a GitHub team so ownership survives someone leaving:
#
#   /db/    @your-org/security
#   /lib/   @your-org/security
#
# Teams only work inside an organisation; on a personal account, list handles individually
# (space-separated) and note that GitHub requires only ONE of them to approve, not all.
